WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.960
A legendary sax player, an&nbsp;
iconic sitcom grandfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.820
and the old neighbor from a holiday movie&nbsp;
classic are just a few of the stars who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.901
had memorable turns in Martin Scorsese&nbsp;
films but have sadly since passed away.

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:20.040
Martin Scorsese's first film, Who's That Knocking&nbsp;
At My Door, began as a 1965 student project with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:25.320
Harvey Keitel called Bring on the Dancing Girls. A&nbsp;
subplot involving a young woman with whom Keitel's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.320
character becomes involved was added in 1967.&nbsp;
Actress and dancer Zina Bethune played Keitel's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.540
love interest, whose complicated past proves too&nbsp;
difficult for his Catholic-influenced upbringing.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.320
Bethune began performing at an early&nbsp;
age, despite suffering from scoliosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.340
a condition that causes curvature of the&nbsp;
spine. She danced with the New York City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.440
Ballet when she was a teenager and began&nbsp;
acting on Broadway and TV in the late 1950s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.860
including series regular work&nbsp;
on the daytime soap The Nurses.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.660
After Who's That Knocking At My Door, Bethune&nbsp;
returned to TV and later formed her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.680
nonprofit dance company called Theater&nbsp;
Bethune, which enrolled children with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.960
disabilities in dance-related projects. Five&nbsp;
days before her 67th birthday on February 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.940
2012, she was driving in Los Angeles and&nbsp;
exited her car to assist an injured animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.434
by the side of the road. She was struck by&nbsp;
oncoming traffic and died at the age of 66.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:21.720
After legendary producer Roger Corman&nbsp;
saw Who's That Knocking On My Door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.780
he hired Scorcese to direct what would become&nbsp;
1972's Boxcar Bertha, a Depression Era-drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.640
about a Southern girl turned bank and train&nbsp;
robber. Corman also connected Scorsese with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.740
David Carradine to star as "Big" Bill Shelley,&nbsp;
a union organizer who joins Bertha on her crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.060
spree. Carradine would later reunite with&nbsp;
Scorsese for a cameo in Mean Streets, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.380
his unnamed character is shot in the back by a man&nbsp;
played by Carradine's real-life brother, Robert.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.240
Carradine appeared in various movies and&nbsp;
TV shows before achieving widespread fame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:57.480
on the 70s action series Kung Fu. The actor's&nbsp;
eccentric personality, countercultural leanings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.100
and run-ins with the law took him out of the&nbsp;
mainstream and into low-budget features like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.920
Death Race 2000 and Q – The Winged Serpent.&nbsp;
He enjoyed a brief career revival as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:15.540
titular evil final boss in both volumes of Kill&nbsp;
Bill, which came out in 2003 and 2004. In 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:21.180
Carradine was found dead from asphyxiation&nbsp;
in a hotel room in Thailand; he was 72.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:27.240
In a pivotal scene in Scorsese's 1976 feature&nbsp;
Taxi Driver, Robert De Niro's Travis Bickle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.940
confesses to a veteran cabbie named Wizard that&nbsp;
he has an urge to do something drastic. Wizard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.480
played by Peter Boyle, listens to&nbsp;
the younger man and attempts to pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.760
along some advice about separating&nbsp;
one's work life from personal time.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.980
"Don't worry so much!"

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:49.800
But as viewers soon discover, those worries&nbsp;
quickly turn Travis from a recluse into a violent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:55.440
dangerous individual with delusions of heroism.&nbsp;
As for Peter Boyle, he was a popular character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.320
actor and occasional leading man in the 70s,&nbsp;
in the likes of such films as 1970's Joe. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.780
also memorably showed off his comedy chops&nbsp;
as the monster in 1974's Young Frankenstein.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.780
Boyle transitioned to TV in the 80s and the 90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.400
including his Emmy-nominated turn as&nbsp;
the gleefully cranky Frank Barone on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.320
the long-running sitcom Everybody Loves Raymond.&nbsp;
That led to a turn in the Oscar-nominated drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:26.324
Monster's Ball in 2001 before his death&nbsp;
from multiple myeloma in 2006. He was 71.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.540
Clarence Clemons was best known as the&nbsp;
saxophone player in Bruce Springsteen's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.620
E Street Band. But he also recorded&nbsp;
several solo albums and collaborated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.760
with dozens of other performers,&nbsp;
including Lady Gaga, Aretha Franklin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.320
and Jackson Browne. He also enjoyed a&nbsp;
second career as an actor, including a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.660
brief appearance in Scorsese's 1977 film New&nbsp;
York, New York as trumpet player Cecil Powell.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:53.640
New York, New York was Clemons' first turn as&nbsp;
an actor, and it led to guest star appearances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.800
on shows like Diff'rent Strokes. In 2004, he&nbsp;
showed off his dramatic skills on two episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.960
of The Wire as a Baltimore youth program&nbsp;
coordinator. He also memorably played One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.740
of the Three Most Important People in&nbsp;
the World in Bill and Ted's Excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:13.920
Adventure. Clemons died of complications from&nbsp;
a stroke on June 18, 2011 at the age of 69.

00:04:14.134 --> 00:04:17.880
Theresa Saldana enjoyed a&nbsp;
long career in movies and TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.680
including playing Joe Pesci's wife in&nbsp;
1980's Raging Bull. But that was all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:27.480
overshadowed by an incident of real-life&nbsp;
violence that nearly left her dead.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:33.300
Saldana rose to fame as a Beatles fan in 1978's&nbsp;
I Wanna Hold Your Hand a couple of years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.880
Raging Bull. Two years after the latter film,&nbsp;
she was assaulted by a deranged admirer in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.260
of her home in West Hollywood, California. The&nbsp;
attacker had obtained her personal information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:48.060
through a private investigator and even spoke to&nbsp;
Saldana's mother in order to locate her address.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:53.760
After four months of recovery, Saldana returned&nbsp;
to acting, playing herself in the 1984 TV movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.460
Victims for Victims: The Theresa Saldana Story and&nbsp;
earning a Golden Globe nomination as the wife of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.980
Michael Chiklis on The Commish. She also became&nbsp;
a dedicated activist, founding the Victims for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.080
Victims support organization and lobbying for&nbsp;
anti-stalking legislation. She continued to act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:17.267
on TV until 2004 and then died in 2016 at the age&nbsp;
of 61 following a hospitalization for pneumonia.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:20.701
"She was an example of someone who the&nbsp;
worst thing in the world happened to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:25.382
gathered her strength, and worked not only on&nbsp;
behalf of herself, but on behalf of others."

00:05:25.382 --> 00:05:30.240
1982's The King of Comedy pitted Robert De&nbsp;
Niro's would-be stand-up comic Rupert Pupkin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.860
against his idol, talk show host Jerry&nbsp;
Langford, played by legendary comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.560
Jerry Lewis. Lewis rose to fame in parts that&nbsp;
showed off his knack for extreme physical humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.380
but King of Comedy gave him a feature-length&nbsp;
showcase for his considerable dramatic talents.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.320
One of the most celebrated comic actors of the&nbsp;
20th century, Lewis was both a solo star and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.080
part of a hugely popular duo act with Dean Martin.&nbsp;
But at the height of their fame in the late 50s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.100
Lewis broke off on his own in a&nbsp;
series of broadly slapstick but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.180
carefully constructed features, like&nbsp;
The Nutty Professor and The Bellboy.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:12.120
Lewis was also the highly visible face of the&nbsp;
Muscular Dystrophy Association, and hosted its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:19.080
annual fundraising telethon from 1966 to 2010.&nbsp;
Alas, he was also a controversial figure who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:24.960
spoke disparagingly about female comics and after&nbsp;
his death was accused of sexual misconduct by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:31.442
several co-stars. He died at his home in Las&nbsp;
Vegas on August 20, 2017 at the age of 91.

00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:38.400
The eclectic cast of 1988's The Last Temptation of&nbsp;
Christ included Harry Dean Stanton, David Bowie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.440
and Willem Dafoe as the titular messiah. A&nbsp;
familiar face also popped up briefly as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.720
elderly man at a monastery visited by Jesus where&nbsp;
a funeral for an equally venerable master took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.960
place. The master foreshadows the vision Jesus has&nbsp;
of his life if he hadn't been named the Messiah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:01.380
That part was played by character actor Roberts&nbsp;
Blossom, who's perhaps best known as the lonely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:06.886
Old Man Marley in 1990's Home Alone, but he&nbsp;
also enjoyed a long career on stage and screen.

00:07:07.249 --> 00:07:11.868
"We'll see what happens. Merry Christmas."

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:16.920
A veteran of World War II, Blossom worked as&nbsp;
a therapist before landing roles in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.480
theater productions in the 60s. TV and&nbsp;
film soon followed, for which the tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:27.420
rangy Blossom invariably played intense men&nbsp;
who were years or even decades beyond his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:32.460
actual age. This included an inmate who cut&nbsp;
off his own fingers in Escape from Alcatraz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:38.760
and the sinister owner of the titular car in&nbsp;
Christine. His most alarming screen role, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:46.200
was as a psychopath in 1974's Deranged, inspired&nbsp;
by the crimes of real-life serial killer Ed Gein.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.880
Home Alone, meanwhile, showcased Blossom's&nbsp;
versatility in gentler roles. He retired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.460
from acting after his turn in the 1995 Western&nbsp;
The Quick and the Dead, devoting his time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:02.935
poetry instead. He died at the age of 87 on&nbsp;
July 8, 2011 from cerebrovascular disease.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.460
Among the actors considered for the role of&nbsp;
Henry Hill in 1990's Goodfellas were Tom Cruise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:13.440
Sean Penn, and Alec Baldwin. However, it&nbsp;
ultimately went to then-relative unknown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.760
Ray Liotta, after Robert De Niro&nbsp;
saw his harrowing turn in 1986's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:22.560
Something Wild. To prepare for his Goodfellas&nbsp;
performance, Liotta listened to the FBI's audio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.101
recordings of Hill in order to capture&nbsp;
his matter-of-fact delivery and accent.

00:08:27.252 --> 00:08:28.872
"Now take me to jail."

00:08:28.872 --> 00:08:33.540
Though perhaps best known for rough-hewn,&nbsp;
hot-wired types like Hill and in films like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:37.320
Narc and The Many Saints of Newark,&nbsp;
Liotta played a diverse array of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.100
characters throughout his long career. As&nbsp;
he put it to Long Island Weekly in 2018,

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:46.080
"I've done movies with the Muppets. I did&nbsp;
Sinatra [...] I did a movie with an elephant."

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:51.360
Other outside-the-box turns for Liotta included&nbsp;
a flashy divorce lawyer in Marriage Story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.080
an FBI chief in Hannibal, a&nbsp;
dirty police officer in Cop Land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:58.500
and a survivor of the Alamo in&nbsp;
the miniseries Texas Rising.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.760
Liotta worked at a breathless pace&nbsp;
for decades, moving between features,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.880
TV shows, and voice-over performances for animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.560
projects and video games. At the time&nbsp;
of his death at the age of 67 on May 26,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:16.560
2022, he had at least five posthumous films in&nbsp;
various states of completion set to come out.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:23.880
When Scorsese decided to remake the 1962 thriller&nbsp;
Cape Fear in 1991, he paid homage to the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:29.280
film in a number of ways. Chief among these&nbsp;
was his use of composer Bernard Herrmann's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.660
chilling score, while he also tapped three&nbsp;
of the original film's stars to play minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.260
roles in his version. Gregory Peck, who&nbsp;
played lawyer Sam Bowden in the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:42.840
this time played the jailhouse lawyer who helps&nbsp;
to free Robert De Niro's Max Cady. And presiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.620
over the trial was Martin Balsam, who played&nbsp;
police lieutenant Mark Dutton in the original.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:50.940
And then there was Robert Mitchum, whose&nbsp;
performance as Max Cady in the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:56.460
was selected as the 28th greatest cinematic&nbsp;
villain of all time by the American Film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:01.500
Institute. For Scorcese's version, he played&nbsp;
the sympathetic but cagey Lieutenant Elgart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:05.460
who realized that attempts to scare off&nbsp;
Cady with legal threats were useless.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.360
A memorable presence in films&nbsp;
from the 40s through the 90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:15.060
Mitchum radiated effortless cool in a wide&nbsp;
variety of projects, from classic noir like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:20.580
Out of the Past to epic miniseries like The&nbsp;
Winds of War. No matter what the role required,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:25.860
Mitchum always delivered while also managing&nbsp;
to seem as if the task was no big deal.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.120
Mitchum was also a roguish presence&nbsp;
off-screen. He famously recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:35.220
an album of calypso music in 1957,&nbsp;
and essentially laughed off 43 days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.600
in prison for possession of marijuana in&nbsp;
1948. A heavy smoker throughout his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.900
he died of emphysema and lung cancer about a&nbsp;
month before his 80th birthday on July 1, 1997.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:51.960
At the heart of Scorsese's 1993 adaptation of&nbsp;
Edith Wharton's novel The Age of Innocence was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:57.000
the stifling atmosphere of 19th century New&nbsp;
York, where the rules of etiquette prevent its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:01.800
characters from expressing their true feelings.&nbsp;
Wharton used a love triangle to illustrate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.880
situation, with lawyer Newland Archer falling&nbsp;
hopelessly in love with a divorced American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:10.680
countess, yet unable to act on his feelings due&nbsp;
to his engagement to her cousin. In the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:14.220
Archer is thrust into the situation&nbsp;
against his will by his employer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:18.372
Mr. Letterblair, who was played&nbsp;
by veteran actor Norman Lloyd.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:23.534
"Have you considered the consequences&nbsp;
if the countess decides for divorce?"

00:11:23.534 --> 00:11:29.160
Lloyd was perhaps best known to modern audiences&nbsp;
for his long run as a doctor on St. Elsewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:35.160
but his career reaches back to the 1930s, when he&nbsp;
was a member of multiple esteemed stage companies.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:41.220
Appearances in the Alfred Hitchcock films Saboteur&nbsp;
and Spellbound led to a stint as director and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:46.440
associate producer of the anthology series Alfred&nbsp;
Hitchcock Presents. Lloyd continued to act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:52.320
produce, and direct into his ninth decade,&nbsp;
amassing credits that ranged from Dead Poets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:58.320
Society to the Amy Schumer comedy Trainwreck.&nbsp;
In 2007, his long and storied career was even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:07.140
the subject of a documentary called Who is Norman&nbsp;
Lloyd? On May 11, 2021, he died at the age of 106.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:13.860
The Aviator, Scorsese's 2004 biopic of Howard&nbsp;
Hughes, was filled with major talent in virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:18.900
every role. Leonardo DiCaprio took the lead,&nbsp;
Cate Blanchett played Katharine Hepburn in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:24.900
Oscar-winning turn, and the likes of Alan Alda,&nbsp;
Alec Baldwin, and Gwen Stefani also appeared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:30.000
Also showing up briefly was Edward Herrmann as&nbsp;
Joseph Breen, the head of the Production Code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.740
Administration, which censored content for&nbsp;
movies, including Hughes' notorious Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:40.260
The Outlaw. Herrmann later reunited with&nbsp;
Scorsese for 2013's The Wolf of Wall Street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:45.480
lending his measured tones to a commercial for&nbsp;
the fictitious brokerage firm Stratton Oakmont.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:53.100
"Stratton Oakmont: Stability. Integrity. Pride."

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:59.040
Herrmann is probably best remembered as Lorelai&nbsp;
Gilmore's patrician father Richard on Gilmore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.140
Girls, but he has a long list of onscreen&nbsp;
credits reaching back to the early 70s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:08.880
He began his career on stage before his big&nbsp;
break in the miniseries Eleanor and Franklin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:13.920
for which he earned an Emmy nomination&nbsp;
as President Franklin D. Roosevelt. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.420
turn largely cemented his reputation for&nbsp;
playing well-to-do authority figures. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:23.580
played FDR again in the 1982 movie version&nbsp;
of Annie, and he also played well-heeled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:27.300
slightly stuffy figures in films&nbsp;
like Overboard and Richie Rich.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.680
Herrmann also lent his voice to numerous&nbsp;
commercials and documentaries, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:37.440
Ken Burns' The Roosevelts: An Intimate Series&nbsp;
and Cancer: The Emperor of All Maladies. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:41.520
latter project was his final credit. He&nbsp;
was suffering from the disease himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:47.040
as he died from brain cancer at&nbsp;
the age of 71 on December 31, 2014.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:53.220
Christopher Lee didn't have a whole lot of screen&nbsp;
time in 2011's Hugo, but his character of Monsieur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:58.440
Labisse was a crucial component of the film's&nbsp;
central mystery. A bookseller by trade, Labisse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:03.180
is kind to the film's female protagonist Isabelle,&nbsp;
lending her books that help her understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:08.100
world around her. He later directs Isabelle and&nbsp;
her orphan friend Hugo to the Film Academy Library&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:13.380
when they suspect that Isabelle's godfather is&nbsp;
really the silent film pioneer Georges Melies.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:18.900
At the time of Hugo's release, Lee was enjoying a&nbsp;
surge of popularity even greater than his tenure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:24.120
at England's Hammer Films in the 60s. That was&nbsp;
when he played virtually every movie monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:30.780
imaginable, including Frankenstein's monster,&nbsp;
Dracula, and the Mummy. A magnetic presence thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:36.780
to his considerable height and distinctive&nbsp;
voice, he was a go-to-actor for horror and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:42.480
suspense for decades. But he chafed under such&nbsp;
typecasting and headed for Hollywood in the 70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:46.560
where he stood out in films like the James&nbsp;
Bond flick The Man with the Golden Gun.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:51.960
A generation of filmmakers who grew up with&nbsp;
Lee's early performances were delighted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:56.220
the opportunity to cast him in their own&nbsp;
films during the new millennium. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:02.220
a pair of blockbuster franchises, Peter Jackson's&nbsp;
Lord of the Rings trilogy and George Lucas' Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:07.740
Wars prequels, introduced Lee to a whole&nbsp;
new generation. An astonishingly prolific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:13.200
actor with hundreds of film credits to his&nbsp;
name, he remained active until 2015 while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:18.480
also enjoying a surprising second career as&nbsp;
a heavy metal recording artist. On June 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:23.640
2015, the 93-year-old Lee died of&nbsp;
respiratory problems and heart failure.

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:29.940
In Hugo, Mama Jeanne, played by Helen McCrory,&nbsp;
is the kindly spouse of Ben Kingsley's toy store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:35.880
owner Georges. And like her husband, she has a&nbsp;
remarkable secret. She's actually Jehanne d'Alcy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:40.800
a real-life French actress and wife of&nbsp;
director Georges Melies. She also appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.634
in many of Melies' most influential&nbsp;
films, including A Trip to the Moon.

00:15:44.738 --> 00:15:47.515
"There are things you're too young to understand.&nbsp;
You should not yet know such sadness."

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:56.520
McCrory began her career on the London&nbsp;
stage before progressing into films and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:01.560
TV. She made her screen debut with a minor&nbsp;
role in 1994's Interview with the Vampire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:06.960
and soon moved up to supporting characters in&nbsp;
the likes of 2002's The Count of Monte Cristo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:13.320
A turn as Prime Minister Tony Blair's wife&nbsp;
Cherie in 2006's The Queen preceded her debut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:19.260
as Narcissa Malfoy in the Harry Potter series&nbsp;
and high-profile projects like Peaky Blinders.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:24.600
McCrory also turned up in the 2012 James&nbsp;
Bond flick Skyfall and lent her voice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:31.200
2009's Fantastic Mr. Fox. On April 16,&nbsp;
2021, her husband, actor Damien Lewis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:36.060
announced via social media that she'd&nbsp;
died of breast cancer at the age of 52.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:41.820
In addition to his regular lead actors like Robert&nbsp;
De Niro, Harvey Keitel, and Leonardo DiCaprio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:47.700
Scorsese also tapped a small pool of supporting&nbsp;
players to appear in many of his projects. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:52.740
his earliest regular collaborators was Victor&nbsp;
Argo, who appeared in small but notable roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:58.020
in Boxcar Bertha, Mean Streets, and Taxi&nbsp;
Driver. After a gap of about a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:03.000
Argo then reunited with Scorsese for After&nbsp;
Hours and The Last Temptation of Christ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.960
as well as the "Life Lessons" segment&nbsp;
of the anthology film New York Stories.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:12.360
Argo was a familiar face to filmgoers for more&nbsp;
than three decades, thanks to character roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:17.280
in dozens of Hollywood and indie features.&nbsp;
Director Abel Ferrara featured him in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:22.020
number of his projects, including King of New&nbsp;
York and Bad Lieutenant, as did Woody Allen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:27.660
who tapped Argo for Crimes and Misdemeanors and&nbsp;
Shadows and Fog. The actor also played his share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:33.060
of tough guys in films like Raw Deal and True&nbsp;
Romance, and he was also adept at sympathetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:37.680
types in the likes of 2001's Angel Eyes,&nbsp;
which cast him as Jennifer Lopez's father.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:44.520
Argo died at the age of 69 on April 7, 2004&nbsp;
from complications of lung cancer. Scorsese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.240
filmed a short tribute that aired at a&nbsp;
memorial for the actor. His description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.920
of Argo as a wise and dependable&nbsp;
soul was echoed by many of his fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:57.900
and by admirers like author and film historian&nbsp;
Richard Harland Smith, who told Looper,

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:02.280
"Vic rarely did better than seventh&nbsp;
or eighth billing in the movies but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:07.260
offscreen he was a star. He had a huge&nbsp;
following in the New York theater world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:13.620
he was absolutely adored. He was generous with&nbsp;
his company and his time. He loved to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:16.980
to workshop new stuff, even if&nbsp;
he wouldn't get paid for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:22.740
and he loved to encourage underrepresented&nbsp;
talent. He made us all feel important."

